Lexonym helps companies secure and govern their use of generative AI without forcing them to replace the tools and subscriptions they already use.

As employees increasingly adopt ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and other models, new risks emerge: sensitive data exposure, uncontrolled usage, provider sprawl, limited visibility for IT teams, and difficulty enforcing company security policies.

Lexonym adds an independent control layer between the organization and the AI models it uses.

The solution enables companies to:

- detect and protect sensitive data before it is exposed to a model;
- analyze prompts and documents;
- detect prompt injection and jailbreak attempts;
- enforce security and governance policies;
- control which models and use cases are authorized;
- give IT teams visibility into generative AI usage;
- preserve the freedom to choose and switch between model providers.

Companies keep their own subscriptions and direct relationships with AI providers. Lexonym secures and governs these existing usages without locking them into a new ecosystem.

Designed and developed in France, Lexonym promotes a pragmatic approach to digital sovereignty: enabling organizations to benefit from the best AI models on the market while retaining control over their data, usage policies and technology choices.

